- How would you verify stability with duplicates in this approach?
- How would you adapt to 64-bit integers and different memory constraints?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to combine hash maps, deques, and heaps to implement a sliding-window top-K feature in real time.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window with per-player aggregates
- Lazy deletions in a heap to avoid frequent rebalancing
- Expiration management via per-player deques
- Trade-offs between memory usage and update/query latency

## Code Example
```javascript
class Leaderboard {
  constructor(W) {
    this.W = W;
    this.map = new Map();
    this.heap = [];
  }

  addEvent(pid, delta, ts) {
    const record = this.map.get(pid) || { sum: 0, deque: [] };
    record.deque.push({ ts, delta });
    record.sum += delta;

    while (record.deque.length && record.deque[0].ts < ts - this.W) {
      record.sum -= record.deque.shift().delta;
    }

    this.map.set(pid, record);
    this.heap.push({ sum: record.sum, pid });
  }

  topK(now, K) {
    const results = [];
    for (let i = 0; i < K; i++) {
      while (this.heap.length) {
        const top = this.heap[0];
        const record = this.map.get(top.pid);
        if (!record || record.sum !== top.sum) {
          heapPop(this.heap);
        } else {
          results.push(this.heapPop(this.heap).pid);
          break;
        }
      }
    }
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Data Structure Overview
| Component | Purpose | Complexity |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| Hash Map | O(1) lookup per player | O(n) memory |
| Per-player Deque | Track event history, expire old entries | O(1) amortized push/shift |
| Max-Heap | Maintain top players efficiently | O(log n) push, O(1) peek |

## Time & Space Complexity
- **addEvent**: O(log n) amortized—each event enters and leaves the deque at most once
- **topK**: O(K log n)—each invalid entry is popped at most once across all queries
- **Memory**: O(n) for the hash map and deques; O(n) worst-case for the heap

## Follow-up Questions
- How to tighten the lazy deletion cost when the heap grows large?
- Can a segment tree or Fenwick tree provide stricter time guarantees?
- How would you handle concurrent updates from multiple streams?

## Why Asked
This question evaluates understanding of fundamental sorting algorithms and their practical trade-offs in real-world applications.

## Key Concepts
- Divide and conquer strategies
- Partitioning vs merging approaches
- In-place vs auxiliary space requirements
- Algorithm stability and performance characteristics

## Code Example
```
// Quicksort partition
function partition(arr, low, high) {
  const pivot = arr[high];
  let i = low - 1;
  for (let j = low; j < high; j++) {
    if (arr[j] < pivot) {
      i++;
      [arr[i], arr[j]] = [arr[j], arr[i]];
    }
  }
  [arr[i + 1], arr[high]] = [arr[high], arr[i + 1]];
  return i + 1;
}

// Mergesort merge
function merge(left, right) {
  const result = [];
  let i = 0, j = 0;

  while (i < left.length && j < right.length) {
    if (left[i] <= right[j]) {
      result.push(left[i++]);
    } else {
      result.push(right[j++]);
    }
  }

  return result.concat(left.slice(i)).concat(right.slice(j));
}
```

Why This Is Asked\n\nThis question probes understanding of practical sorting trade-offs beyond textbook asymptotics, including cache locality, in-place constraints, and robust pivot strategies. It invites discussion of 3-way partitioning to handle duplicates, median-of-three to curb worst-case behavior, and a small insertion sort for tiny partitions.\n\n## Key Concepts\n- 3-way partitioning in quicksort\n- median-of-three pivot selection\n- insertion sort threshold tuning\n- in-place algorithms and cache efficiency\n- iterative vs recursive implementations\n\n## Code Example\n```javascript
function sortInPlace(arr){
  const TH = 16;
  const swap=(a,i,j)=>{const t=a[i]; a[i]=a[j]; a[j]=t;};
  const medianOfThree=(a,i,j,k)=>{let x=[a[i],i],[a[j],j],[a[k],k].sort((p,q)=>p[0]-q[0]); return a[x[1]];};
  // iterative 3-way quicksort skeleton
  const stack=[[0,arr.length-1]];
  while(stack.length){
    const [lo,hi]=stack.pop();
    if(hi-lo+1<=TH){ // insertion sort for small partitions
      for(let i=lo+1;i<=hi;i++){
        let t=arr[i], j=i-1;
        while(j>=lo && arr[j]>t){ arr[j+1]=arr[j]; j-- }
        arr[j+1]=t;
      }
      continue;
    }
    // median-of-three pivot
    const mid=(lo+hi)>>1;
    const pivot= medianOfThree(arr, lo, mid, hi);
    // 3-way partition
    let lt=lo, i=lo, gt=hi;
    while(i<=gt){
      if(arr[i]<pivot) swap(arr, lt++, i++);
      else if(arr[i]>pivot) swap(arr, i, gt--);
      else i++;
    }
    stack.push([lo, lt-1]);
    stack.push([gt+1, hi]);
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of a Trie, basic operations, and how to implement autocomplete with limited results.

## Key Concepts
- Trie nodes with children and isEnd
- DFS traversal for autocomplete
- Lexicographic ordering via alphabetical traversal

## Code Example
```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.children = new Map();
    this.isEnd = false;
  }
}

class Trie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }
  insert(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) node.children.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    node.isEnd = true;
  }
  search(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of word) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return false;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return node.isEnd;
  }
  startsWith(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return false;
      node = node.children.get(ch);
    }
    return true;
  }
  autocomplete(prefix, limit) {
    let node = this.root; const path = [];
    for (const ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.children.has(ch)) return [];
      node = node.children.get(ch);
      path.push(ch);
    }
    const results = [];
    function dfs(n, acc) {
      if (results.length >= limit) return;
      if (n.isEnd) results.push(prefix + acc.join(''));
      const keys = Array.from(n.children.keys()).sort();
      for (const c of keys) {
        acc.push(c);
        dfs(n.children.get(c), acc);
        acc.pop();
      }
    }
    dfs(node, []);
    return results;
  }
}
```

